# Every text file checks out with LF on every OS, so tests, golden files and byte comparisons
# see the same bytes on Windows as on Linux and macOS. Git detects binary files by content and
# leaves them alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Hooks are shell scripts, which a CRLF checkout would break in Git for Windows.
/.githooks/** text eol=lf

# Binary formats are named, so no line-ending conversion or text diff touches them, even a
# file whose first bytes hold no NUL and would pass Git's content check for text: images,
# model files and raw byte fixtures such as `crates/manifest/tests/data/tiny-header.bin`.
*.png binary
*.gguf binary
*.safetensors binary
*.bin binary

# A fixture that must keep its exact bytes, such as a CRLF or mixed line-ending sample, is
# marked `-text` here with a comment saying why. None does today: every tracked text file is
# LF in the index (`git ls-files --eol`, 2026-10-22).
